{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological mutation is undirected, whereas software diversification in a decentralized network is the result of deliberate (though economically constrained) operator choices.","Biological fitness is selected by a capricious environment, whereas software service reliability is often explicitly validated against shared industry standards, potentially reducing diversity."],"overturn_conditions":["Evidence that independent operators in unregulated markets consistently choose identical dependencies and configurations, leading to widespread monocultures that fail together.","A demonstration that a large, internally redundant service can cheaply and effectively simulate diverse design philosophies within its own architecture."],"shared_structure":["Both systems require diversity to survive selective pressures that would wipe out a monoculture.","In both cases, uncoordinated local optimization leads to correlated vulnerabilities because the same 'best' solution is adopted everywhere.","True resilience depends on variance in design philosophy, not merely on the quantity of redundant components."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["A decentralized network of small services is only more resilient than a single large service if it exhibits true design diversity; otherwise, it is merely a distributed monoculture.","Strictly more internal redundancy in a large service is analogous to having more copies of the same genome: it provides no protection against a disease that targets that genome.","Evidence distinguishing truly independent failures from cryptic common-cause failures would consist of observing systematic differences in the problem-solving approaches and non-functional choices of the surviving nodes, not just differences in their failure rates."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small services is only more resilient than a single large service if it